      "The electric pressure washer worth paying for. The induction motor is the whole reason, and it will outlast three cheap machines.",
    description:
      "Almost every complaint about electric pressure washers, that they are loud, that they get hot, that they die after two summers, traces to the universal motor most of them use. The RY142300 uses a brushless induction motor instead, and the difference is immediately obvious. It was the quietest machine in our entire test group, gas or electric, running at a level where conversation nearby is easy. Over extended sessions it showed none of the power fade that heat-soaked universal motors produce, and the housing stayed cool enough to touch. At 2300 PSI it sits at the higher end of the electric range, and in our testing it cleaned vinyl siding, a weathered wood deck, a cedar fence, and patio furniture without difficulty. On concrete it works, though the 1.2 GPM water volume means a driveway takes real time, and a surface cleaner attachment is close to mandatory if you plan on that job regularly. The on-board storage is a genuinely useful design touch, keeping the hose, cord, wand, and all five nozzles on the unit rather than scattered across a garage shelf. The trade-offs are throughput and price. Water volume is the specification electric machines cannot escape, and 1.2 GPM is modest even for the category. It also costs meaningfully more than budget electric units, which is exactly the point: you are paying for a motor that lasts.",
  },
  {
    id: "greenworks-gpw3000-electric",
    name: "Greenworks Pro GPW3000 3000 PSI",
    category: "3000 PSI, 2.0 GPM, Induction Motor, Steel Frame",
    badge: "Most Powerful",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 6800,
    specs: {
      motor: "Induction",
      pump: "Axial cam",
      psi: "3000 PSI",
      gpm: "2.0 GPM",
      cleaningUnits: "≈6,000",
      weight: "~65 lb",
      warranty: "3 years",
      price: "$350-430",
    },
    pros: [
      "3000 PSI at 2.0 GPM gives 6,000 cleaning units, which genuinely cleans concrete",
      "Induction motor for durability and lower noise than universal motor machines",
      "Steel frame and larger wheels that roll over grass and gravel properly",
      "Closes most of the practical gap to entry-level gas machines",
      "No engine to maintain, fuel to store, or oil to change",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Draws enough current that a dedicated 20-amp circuit is strongly advisable",
      "Heavier and bulkier than typical electric pressure washers",
      "Still slower than gas on large flat areas because of lower water volume",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The electric machine for people who want to clean concrete without owning an engine. It genuinely does the job.",
    description:
      "The standard argument for gas is that electric machines cannot handle concrete, and the GPW3000 is the clearest counterexample in this guide. At 3000 PSI and 2.0 gallons per minute it produces 6,000 cleaning units, which puts it in the range of entry-level gas machines and roughly double what a typical budget electric unit manages. In our driveway testing it actually removed embedded algae and dirt rather than merely brightening the surface, which is where lower-output electric machines run out of capability. Build quality supports the output. The steel frame and larger wheels make it genuinely movable across a lawn or gravel drive, unlike the small plastic-framed electric units that have to be carried. The induction motor gives it the quiet operation and long service life that separate serious electric machines from disposable ones. For a homeowner who wants a driveway-capable washer but has no interest in owning a small engine, this is the most direct answer available. Two practical points. It draws substantial current, and sharing a circuit with other loads will trip breakers, so a dedicated 20-amp circuit or a heavy-gauge cord on its own circuit is worth arranging before the first use. And despite the pressure, 2.0 GPM is still below any gas machine, so a surface cleaner attachment is how you close the remaining speed gap on flat concrete.",
  },
  {
    id: "sun-joe-spx3000-electric",
    name: "Sun Joe SPX3000 2030 PSI",
    category: "2030 PSI, 1.76 GPM, Universal Motor, Dual Tanks",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 47200,
    specs: {
      motor: "Universal",
      pump: "Axial cam",
      psi: "2030 PSI",
      gpm: "1.76 GPM",
      cleaningUnits: "≈3,570",
