      "Two onboard detergent tanks let you switch soaps with a dial rather than draining and refilling",
      "Total Stop System cuts the pump when you release the trigger, which extends pump life",
      "Light and compact enough to carry with one hand and store on a shelf",
      "Five quick-connect nozzles included covering every common task",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Universal motor is loud and has a considerably shorter service life than induction",
      "Almost entirely plastic construction, including the wheels and frame",
      "Runs hot during extended sessions, with noticeable power fade",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Cheap, popular, and genuinely functional. Buy it knowing it is a few-seasons machine rather than a decade-long one.",
    description:
      "The SPX3000 has been among the best-selling pressure washers in the country for years, and the reasons are straightforward: it costs a fraction of a gas machine, it produces enough pressure and water volume to clean siding, decks, fences, furniture, and vehicles effectively, and it is small enough to store anywhere. For a homeowner who pressure washes a handful of times a year, that covers the real demand at minimal cost. Its dual detergent tanks are genuinely unusual at this price. Keeping a house wash in one tank and a degreaser in the other, and switching between them with a dial rather than draining and rinsing, is more convenient than most machines costing several times as much. The Total Stop System is the other important feature, cutting the pump when the trigger releases, which prevents the heat buildup that destroys pumps left running in bypass. The honest limitation is the motor. This uses a universal motor, which is why it is loud, why it heats up during long sessions with noticeable power fade, and why its expected service life is measured in a few seasons of regular use rather than a decade. The plastic frame and wheels reflect the same economics. Framed as an inexpensive tool with a finite life, it is a reasonable purchase. Framed as a long-term investment, it is not.",
  },
  {
    id: "karcher-k5-electric",
    name: "Karcher K5 Premium Smart Control",
    category: "2000 PSI, 1.4 GPM, Water-Cooled Induction",
    badge: "Best for Cars",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 11400,
    specs: {
      motor: "Water-cooled induction",
      pump: "Axial cam",
      psi: "2000 PSI",
      gpm: "1.4 GPM",
      cleaningUnits: "≈2,800",
      weight: "~35 lb",
      warranty: "3 years",
      price: "$300-380",
    },
    pros: [
      "Water-cooled induction motor runs cool and quiet with excellent expected longevity",
      "Smart Control sets appropriate pressure per surface, making damage far less likely",
      "The best foam cannon performance in this guide for washing vehicles",
      "Integrated hose reel keeps the high-pressure hose from becoming a tangle",
      "Gentle enough for painted surfaces and delicate materials at its lower settings",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Lower cleaning units than the more powerful electric units, so concrete is slow",
      "Karcher uses a proprietary accessory connection rather than standard quick-connect",
      "Premium pricing within the electric category",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The safest electric pressure washer to hand to a first-time user, and the best one for vehicles. Pressure control is the feature.",
    description:
      "Most pressure washer damage happens because the operator applied more pressure than the surface could tolerate, and Karcher Smart Control addresses that directly. You choose the surface, siding, wood, car, or masonry, and the machine sets an appropriate pressure automatically and displays it on the wand. In our testing it was the machine least likely to cause accidental damage, and the one we would hand to someone who has never used a pressure washer before without a long safety briefing. For vehicles it is the standout in this guide. Its foam cannon produces thick, clinging foam that dwells on paint long enough to lift road film before rinsing, and its available pressure levels are appropriate for automotive paint rather than dangerous to it. The water-cooled induction motor keeps noise low and expected service life high, and the integrated hose reel solves the perennial problem of a stiff high-pressure hose ending up in a tangled pile. Two limitations. Its 2000 PSI at 1.4 GPM produces 2,800 cleaning units, which is well below what makes a driveway practical, so this is a machine for vertical surfaces and vehicles rather than large concrete. And Karcher uses a proprietary accessory fitting rather than the standard quick-connect that most machines share, which means aftermarket nozzles and surface cleaners either need an adapter or are unavailable.",
  },
  {
    id: "ego-cordless-pressure-washer",
    name: "EGO Power+ 56V Cordless Pressure Washer",
    category: "Cordless Battery, 3200 PSI Peak, 1.2 GPM",
    badge: "Best Cordless",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 4800,
    specs: {
      motor: "56V brushless",
      pump: "Axial cam",
      psi: "3200 PSI peak",
      gpm: "1.2 GPM",
      cleaningUnits: "≈3,840",
      weight: "~40 lb bare",
      warranty: "5 years",
      price: "$500 bare",
    },
    pros: [
      "Completely cordless, so it works anywhere including places with no outlet at all",
      "Can draw water from a bucket, lake, or rain barrel rather than requiring a spigot",
      "Peak pressure competitive with corded electric machines",
      "Runs on the same 56V batteries as EGO mowers, blowers, and trimmers",
      "Extremely quiet and instantly ready with no cord to route or trip over",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Runtime is limited, typically well under an hour on a large battery",
      "Expensive, particularly once a large battery and charger are included",
      "Lower sustained output than a good corded electric machine",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The only pressure washer that goes anywhere. For boats, remote fences, and job sites without power, nothing else does this.",
    description:
      "Every other machine in this guide needs both a spigot and an outlet, which is a real constraint more often than people expect: a boat on a trailer, a fence at the far end of a property, a cabin, a work site. The EGO cordless pressure washer needs neither. It runs on the same 56V batteries as the rest of the EGO lineup, and it can draw water from a bucket, a barrel, a lake, or any standing source through its intake hose rather than requiring pressurized supply. That combination genuinely enables jobs the others cannot attempt. Peak pressure is competitive with corded electric machines, and in our testing it cleaned patio furniture, a boat hull, and a section of fence effectively. Operation is near silent and there is no cord to route, tangle, or trip over, which makes moving around an object being washed far easier than it sounds. The costs are runtime and price. Battery capacity limits sustained work, and even a large pack does not last a full afternoon of continuous cleaning. It is also expensive, particularly as a kit. And sustained output sits below a good corded machine, because a battery cannot supply the same continuous power a wall outlet can. Buy it for the portability, which is genuinely unmatched, rather than expecting it to outperform a corded unit at a spigot.",
  },
  {
    id: "westinghouse-epx3100",
    name: "Westinghouse ePX3100 2050 PSI",
    category: "2050 PSI, 1.76 GPM, Induction Motor, Compact",
    badge: "Best Value Induction",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 9300,
    specs: {
      motor: "Induction",
      pump: "Axial cam",
      psi: "2050 PSI",
      gpm: "1.76 GPM",
      cleaningUnits: "≈3,610",
      weight: "~24 lb",
      warranty: "3 years",
      price: "$180-230",
    },
    pros: [
      "Induction motor at a price close to universal-motor budget machines",
      "2050 PSI at 1.76 GPM gives a solid 3,600 cleaning units for the money",
      "Very compact vertical design that stores in minimal floor space",
      "Total Stop System and a soap tank included",
      "Quieter and cooler running than budget competitors at similar output",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Compact design makes it slightly tippy when dragging the hose",
      "Shorter power cord than some competitors",
      "Accessory quality is adequate rather than impressive",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The value sweet spot. An induction motor at close to budget pricing means it should outlast the cheap machines by years.",
    description:
      "The most useful thing about the ePX3100 is that it brings an induction motor down to a price point where budget machines with universal motors normally live. That single specification is the strongest predictor of how long an electric pressure washer lasts, and getting it without paying premium pricing is genuinely good value. In our testing it ran noticeably quieter and cooler than the universal-motor machines at comparable output, and it showed none of the power fade that appears in cheap units during extended sessions. At 2050 PSI and 1.76 gallons per minute it produces around 3,600 cleaning units, which handles siding, decks, fences, furniture, and vehicles comfortably and makes light concrete work feasible if slow. The Total Stop System protects the pump by shutting it off when the trigger releases, and a soap tank is integrated rather than requiring a separate siphon setup. The compact vertical design is the other appeal, storing in a fraction of the floor space a horizontal machine needs, which matters in a crowded garage. That same compactness is its main ergonomic weakness: the narrow footprint makes it somewhat tippy when you drag the hose sideways, and it needs repositioning more often than a wider machine. The cord is also shorter than some competitors, and the included accessories are functional rather than notable.",
  },
  {
    id: "dewalt-dwpw2600",
    name: "DeWalt DWPW2600 2600 PSI",
    category: "2600 PSI, 1.1 GPM, CETA-Certified, Stackable Frame",
    badge: "Best Storage & Portability",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 5200,
    specs: {
      motor: "Universal",
      pump: "Axial cam",
      psi: "2600 PSI",
      gpm: "1.1 GPM",
      cleaningUnits: "≈2,860",
      weight: "~40 lb",
      warranty: "3 years",
      price: "$350-430",
    },
    pros: [
      "CETA-certified performance, so the 2600 PSI figure is measured at the nozzle rather than inside the pump",
      "Roll-cage frame stacks and stores in a fraction of the space of an upright cart machine",
      "All five nozzles, the wand, the hose, and the cord store on board with nothing left loose",
      "2600 PSI handles siding, decks, fences, and light concrete without complaint",
      "Also sold in 2100 PSI and 3000 PSI versions if you want a different balance",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Universal motor, so expect a shorter service life than an induction machine",
      "Small wheels struggle on grass, gravel, and bare ground",
      "1.1 GPM is the lowest water volume in this guide, which slows flat concrete work",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best-designed machine here for storage and transport, with a certified pressure rating you can actually trust. Buy it for the packaging, not the motor.",
    description:
      "Most electric pressure washers are designed as though they will live permanently in the middle of a garage floor, and the DWPW2600 is the clearest exception. Its roll-cage cube frame stacks, stores on a shelf, and swallows every accessory: the nozzles clip to the wand, the hose tucks inside the cage, and the cord wraps at the back, which in practice means nothing goes missing between jobs. That sounds like a minor convenience until you compare it against a season of hunting for a black soap nozzle in a bin. The more important point for this guide is the rating. DeWalt publishes its figures as CETA-certified, measured at the nozzle with a reference tip, rather than the internal PSI Max numbers several competitors print on the box. When this machine says 2600 PSI, that is roughly what you get, and in our testing it cleaned vinyl siding, a cedar fence, and a weathered deck at a pace consistent with that figure. Two real limitations. The motor is universal rather than induction, so it is louder, runs hotter, and should be expected to last seasons rather than a decade, which is the same trade the Sun Joe machines make at a lower price. And at 1.1 GPM it moves the least water of anything here, so flat concrete is slow going and a surface cleaner is close to mandatory if a driveway is on your list. The small wheels also do poorly on grass and gravel, though at around 40 pounds it is light enough to simply carry.",
