        <div id="what-electric-does-well" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Electric Pressure Washers Do Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric pressure washers are not simply weaker gas machines. They are better at several specific jobs, and knowing which ones prevents both disappointment and unnecessary spending on gas equipment.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Vertical surfaces are their natural home.</strong> Siding, fences, garage doors, and exterior walls all want moderate pressure and careful control rather than raw volume. An electric machine at 2000 to 2300 PSI cleans them effectively without the risk that a high-output gas machine brings, and being quiet enough to hear yourself think while working makes the job less unpleasant.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Vehicles are safer with electric.</strong> Automotive paint tolerates roughly 1,500 PSI, and a gas machine at 3,000 PSI is a tool you must actively restrain. Electric machines land naturally in the right range, and several offer surface-specific pressure control that makes damage genuinely unlikely.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Frequent short jobs favor electric.</strong> Cleaning patio furniture, rinsing a walkway, or washing garbage cans is a five-minute task, and a machine you plug in and switch on gets used for that. A gas machine that needs checking oil and pulling a cord does not, which is why gas washers often sit unused for jobs an electric one would have handled.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Storage and noise matter more than people expect.</strong> An electric unit stores on a shelf, needs no fuel in the garage, and does not require hearing protection. For an apartment, a townhouse, or a home with limited garage space, those practical factors often outweigh cleaning speed entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Where they genuinely fall short:</strong> large flat concrete. Water volume is what carries dirt away and covers area, and 1.2 to 2.0 GPM against a gas machine 2.4 to 3.5 GPM is a gap that shows up directly in hours spent on a driveway.
          </p>

