                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,200-1,500 PSI</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40&deg; white</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vinyl siding, fences</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500-2,000 PSI</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40&deg; or 25&deg;</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wood decks</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,200-1,800 PSI</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25&deg; green, keep moving</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete, brick, pavers</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,000-3,000 PSI</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25&deg; or surface cleaner</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 4: PSI Max, GPM Max, and Certification -->
        <div id="certification" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. PSI Max, GPM Max, and Why Certification Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here is the part of this category that nobody selling you a machine wants to explain. The pressure figure printed in large type on the box is not governed by any law, and some brands measure it in the place where the number comes out highest &mdash; inside the machine, at the pump, with no nozzle fitted &mdash; rather than at the nozzle where the water actually meets the dirt.
          </p>

          <p>
            That figure is usually flagged with an asterisk and the word <strong>Max</strong>: PSI Max, GPM Max. It is not necessarily a lie, but it describes a pressure you will never experience while cleaning anything. Two machines advertised at 3,000 PSI can differ by a wide margin in the real world if one number is certified at the nozzle and the other is an internal maximum.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Two Letters Worth Looking For</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              <strong>PWMA</strong> (Pressure Washer Manufacturers' Association) and <strong>CETA</strong> (Cleaning Equipment Trade Association) both run certification programmes that define how pressure and flow must be measured and reported. A machine carrying either mark has had its numbers verified against a published standard. A machine with neither may still be honest &mdash; it simply has not been checked, so the specification is the manufacturer's word.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>How to check before you buy.</strong> Look on the spec sheet or the box for a PWMA or CETA logo, or the phrase "certified performance". If the listing quotes PSI with an asterisk, find the footnote &mdash; it will usually tell you the figure is measured internally. If pressure and flow are quoted without any nozzle reference and the machine is unusually light for its claimed output, treat the number as optimistic.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Not every uncertified machine is overstating.</strong> Some manufacturers with strong reputations have simply not enrolled particular models in a certification programme, and their figures hold up in testing anyway. Certification is evidence, not the only evidence. But between two machines at the same price where one is certified and one prints a Max figure, the certified one is the safer purchase and it is not close.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Where this bites hardest is the budget shelf.</strong> The gap between advertised and delivered pressure tends to widen as price falls, which is exactly where buyers are most reliant on the printed number. If you are shopping under $200, weight and motor type tell you more than the PSI figure does.
          </p>
        </div>

<!-- SECTION 5: Induction vs Universal Motors: The Spec That Decides Lifespan -->
        <div id="motor-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Induction vs Universal Motors: The Spec That Decides Lifespan
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the most consequential specification on any electric pressure washer, and a large number of product listings do not mention it at all. Knowing how to tell them apart is worth more than comparing PSI figures.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Universal Motors</h3>

          <p>
            Universal motors use carbon brushes and a commutator, spin at very high RPM, and are small, light, and cheap to manufacture. They are also loud, run hot, and wear out as the brushes degrade. In an application like a pressure washer, where the motor runs continuously under load, that heat and wear accumulate quickly. Expect a few seasons of regular use rather than a decade, and expect noticeable power fade partway through a long session as the motor heats up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Induction Motors</h3>

          <p>
            Induction motors have no brushes, spin at lower RPM, and are physically larger and heavier. They run cool, run quiet, and last for thousands of hours. In our testing induction-motor machines were dramatically quieter, showed no power fade over extended sessions, and stayed cool enough to touch. This is the technology used in commercial pressure washers for exactly these reasons.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Tell Which You Are Buying</h3>

          <p>
            If the listing does not say, weight is the best proxy: induction motors are substantially heavier, so a very light machine with high claimed pressure is almost certainly a universal motor. Price is the other indicator, since induction machines cost more to build. Manufacturers that use induction motors advertise it prominently, so silence on the specification generally means universal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Universal Motor Is Fine</h3>

          <p>
            If you will use the machine a handful of times a year for short sessions, a universal motor at a low price is a defensible choice, and the money saved is real. The mistake is buying one expecting a decade of regular service, which is where the disappointment and the poor reviews come from.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Axial Cam vs Triplex Pumps</h3>

          <p>
            The motor gets the attention, but the pump is what wears out second. Almost every consumer electric machine uses an axial cam pump, where the pistons sit in line with the driveshaft. They are compact, cheap to build, and give years of service in household use, and there is nothing wrong with one. Triplex pumps run three pistons off a crankshaft, run cooler, last far longer under sustained load, and are usually rebuildable rather than disposable &mdash; which is why they appear on commercial machines and almost never below $1,000. If you are cleaning professionally, look for a triplex. If you are cleaning your own house, an axial cam pump treated properly will outlive the motor in front of it.
          </p>

