                <!-- SECTION 6: Corded vs Cordless vs Hybrid vs Wall-Mounted -->
        <div id="power-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Corded vs Cordless vs Hybrid vs Wall-Mounted
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric used to mean one thing. It now covers four quite different machines, and picking the wrong format is a more expensive mistake than picking the wrong pressure rating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Corded</h3>
          <p>
            The default, and still the right answer for most people. Unlimited runtime, the lowest cost per cleaning unit, and nothing to charge. The trade is that you are tethered to both an outlet and a pressurised water source, which around a typical house means dragging an extension cord and a garden hose to wherever you are working.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cordless</h3>
          <p>
            Battery machines have become genuinely capable rather than novelties, and the best of them out-perform mid-range corded units. Two things make them worth the premium: no outlet to find, and a siphon hose that draws from a bucket, lake, or water butt when there is no spigot. The limit is runtime. Expect to manage power modes and swap batteries, and expect a full driveway to be a stretch even on high-capacity packs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hybrid</h3>
          <p>
            A newer format that runs from either mains power or batteries, so you get cordless freedom for quick jobs and unlimited runtime for long ones. It is the most flexible option and the most expensive, and it only makes sense if you already own batteries in that manufacturer's system or intend to.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wall-Mounted</h3>
          <p>
            A permanently installed unit with a retractable hose reel, mounted in a garage or on an exterior wall. Nothing to drag out and nothing to store, which in practice means it gets used for five-minute jobs a cart machine never gets used for. It requires installation and a nearby water and power supply, and it is not going anywhere with you.
          </p>

