          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pressure Washers vs Power Cleaners</h3>
          <p>
            A separate class of tool has appeared that looks like a cordless pressure washer and is not one. Power cleaners &mdash; the Worx Hydroshot and similar &mdash; are self-contained wands that typically produce 300 to 600 PSI, against 1,500 PSI and up for a real pressure washer. Flow can be comparable, which is why they rinse a bike, a kayak, or muddy boots perfectly well, and why they are useless on a driveway. If a listing quotes pressure in the hundreds, you are looking at a power cleaner, whatever the title says.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wheels, Weight, and Where It Lives</h3>
          <p>
            The specification nobody reads and everybody regrets. Small hard casters roll fine on a smooth garage floor and dig in on grass, gravel, and bare ground, which is exactly where you use the machine. Large-diameter or wide wheels roll over rough terrain properly. It is not a deal-breaker on a light machine you can simply carry, but on a 60-pound unit it decides whether the thing gets used. Check the hose length and cord length while you are there: every extra foot is one less trip back to reposition the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

<!-- SECTION 7: Water Supply, Cavitation, and Why Pumps Fail -->
        <div id="water-supply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Water Supply, Cavitation, and Why Pumps Fail
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric pressure washer pumps almost always fail for reasons unrelated to the electrical side, and understanding the water side prevents nearly all of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The pump must never be starved of water.</strong> A pressure washer pump is designed to move a specific volume, and if the supply cannot deliver it, the pump draws air along with water. That produces cavitation, tiny vapor bubbles collapsing violently inside the pump, which erodes seals and valves rapidly. A machine that stutters, pulses, or produces inconsistent pressure is usually cavitating rather than failing electrically.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use a short, wide garden hose from a fully open spigot.</strong> A 5/8-inch or 3/4-inch hose no longer than about 25 feet, connected to a spigot opened all the way, gives the pump the supply it needs. A long, narrow, or kinked hose is the most common cause of supply starvation, and it is completely invisible as a problem until the pump fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Turn the water on before starting the machine.</strong> Starting a pump dry, even briefly, damages seals. Connect the hose, open the spigot fully, squeeze the trigger to purge air from the system until water flows steadily, and only then switch the machine on.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not run in bypass.</strong> With the machine on and the trigger released, water circulates inside the pump and heats up quickly. Sustained bypass is the leading cause of pump failure across all pressure washers. A Total Stop System shuts the machine off automatically when you release the trigger, which is why it is a feature worth insisting on.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clean the inlet screen.</strong> A small filter at the water inlet catches grit that would otherwise reach the pump, and it clogs gradually. A partially blocked screen starves the pump with no symptom other than reduced pressure that people usually blame on the machine wearing out. Pull it and rinse it a few times each season.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Circuit Nobody Checks</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Higher-output electric pressure washers draw enough current to trip a shared 15-amp circuit, particularly at startup. Running one on a circuit that also serves a refrigerator or a garage freezer is a recipe for nuisance trips and, worse, for a freezer quietly thawing. Give a powerful electric washer its own 20-amp circuit, and if you must use an extension cord, use a heavy-gauge outdoor-rated one as short as possible.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Nozzles, Detergents, and Attachments -->
        <div id="nozzles-and-accessories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Nozzles, Detergents, and Attachments
          </h2>

          <p>
            On an electric machine, where you have less raw power to spare, choosing the right nozzle and using detergent properly matter more than they do on a high-output gas unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Nozzle Colors</h3>

          <p>
            Red is 0 degrees and concentrates everything into a pencil jet that damages almost any surface. Yellow is 15 degrees for aggressive work on hard surfaces. Green is 25 degrees, the general-purpose choice. White is 40 degrees for siding, vehicles, and delicate materials. Black is a low-pressure soap nozzle, and it is the only one that will draw detergent through the machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Detergent Only Works at Low Pressure</h3>

          <p>
            The black soap nozzle exists because the detergent siphon only engages at low pressure. Trying to apply soap through a high-pressure tip simply will not draw it, which is the most common reason people conclude their detergent system is broken. Apply soap with the black tip, let it dwell for several minutes without drying, then switch to a rinsing tip.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Let Chemistry Do the Work</h3>

          <p>
            This matters more on electric machines than gas ones. A proper cleaner that dwells on the surface breaks down algae, mildew, and road film chemically, so you need far less pressure to remove it. On a deck or siding, a dedicated cleaner applied first turns a marginal electric machine into an entirely adequate one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Surface Cleaners Close the Concrete Gap</h3>

          <p>
            A surface cleaner is a shrouded disc with rotating jets that you push across flat concrete. It roughly doubles cleaning speed and eliminates the striping that hand-waving a wand always produces. Check that your machine has enough output to spin one, since some require a minimum GPM, and confirm the fitting matches your machine rather than being proprietary.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Extension Wands Beat Ladders</h3>

          <p>
            A telescoping wand lets you reach second-story siding and soffits from the ground. Using a pressure washer from a ladder is genuinely dangerous because of recoil, and an inexpensive extension eliminates that risk entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

                <!-- SECTION 9: Technique and Safety -->
        <div id="technique-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Technique and Safety
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Lower Pressure Is Still Enough to Injure You</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Electric machines are gentler than gas, not harmless. A stream at even 1,500 PSI can cut skin, and a pressure-washer laceration injects water and debris under the surface, which is why these injuries are treated more seriously than they look. Never point a wand at anyone, never hold a hand in front of the nozzle to check flow, wear eye protection and closed-toe shoes, and keep the trigger locked when you are repositioning.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start Wide, Start Far Back</h3>
          <p>
            Begin with the widest nozzle you have, standing roughly ten feet from the surface, and close the distance gradually while watching what the spray does. If the widest tip at close range still is not shifting the dirt, step down one nozzle angle &mdash; not three. This single habit prevents almost every case of etched siding, furred wood, and stripped paint, because you discover the surface's limit before you cross it rather than after.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep the Wand Moving</h3>
          <p>
            Cleaning is a function of dwell time as much as pressure, so a stationary stream removes progressively more material from one spot. Sweep in steady, overlapping passes at a constant distance and angle. Pausing mid-stroke is what produces the tiger-striping on decks and driveways that people blame on the machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Work Top to Bottom, Rinse Bottom to Top</h3>
          <p>
            On siding, apply detergent from the bottom up so it cannot run down dry surfaces and streak, then rinse from the top down so the dirt has somewhere to go. Spraying upward under lap siding drives water behind the boards and into the wall, so keep the stream angled downward or level on vertical surfaces.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stance and Kickback</h3>
          <p>
            A pressure washer pushes back, and more so with narrow tips. Feet shoulder-width apart, one foot slightly forward, knees soft. Reposition yourself and the machine rather than overreaching, and never operate one from a ladder &mdash; the recoil is exactly the force that puts people on the ground. A telescoping extension wand costs less than an emergency-room visit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Not to Spray</h3>
          <p>
            Electrical anything: outdoor outlets, light fixtures, meters, and "weatherproof" speakers, none of which are rated for a directed high-pressure stream. Also avoid air-conditioner fins, window seals and old glazing, roof shingles, lead-painted surfaces, and anything with soft mortar. Plug the machine into a GFCI-protected outlet, and if you use an extension cord, make it a heavy-gauge outdoor-rated one and keep the connection off wet ground.
          </p>
        </div>

<!-- SECTION 10: Storage, Freeze Protection, and Longevity -->
        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Storage, Freeze Protection, and Longevity
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric pressure washers have no engine to winterize, which leads people to assume no winter preparation is needed. The pump still needs protecting, and freezing is the most common way these machines die.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Water left in the pump will crack it.</strong> Water expands as it freezes, and a consumer pump housing is not designed to survive that. A cracked pump is a terminal failure on most electric machines because the pump is not economically replaceable. This is the single most common cause of an electric pressure washer that worked fine in October and does nothing in April.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Run pump antifreeze through it.</strong> After the last use of the season, disconnect the water supply and draw pump saver antifreeze through the machine until it comes out of the wand. This displaces the water and lubricates the seals, which also prevents them from drying out and hardening over months of storage.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store it above freezing if you can.</strong> A heated basement or a conditioned space removes the risk entirely. An unheated garage in a cold climate does not, even if it rarely freezes hard, because a single overnight freeze is enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Coil the high-pressure hose in large loops.</strong> High-pressure hose is stiff and takes a permanent set if coiled tightly or crushed under stored items. A kinked hose can burst under pressure, which is both dangerous and an expensive replacement. Hang it in wide loops rather than piling it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store the wand and nozzles together.</strong> Losing the black soap nozzle is remarkably common, and it is the one you cannot substitute for, since it is the only tip that lets the detergent siphon engage. Machines with on-board nozzle storage largely solve this; if yours does not, a small box on a shelf works.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the cord before each season.</strong> Pressure washers involve water and electricity in close proximity, and a nicked or cracked power cord is a genuine hazard. Inspect the full length, and always plug into a GFCI-protected outlet, which most machines require anyway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 11: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best electric pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Ryobi RY142300 is the best overall, combining 2300 PSI with a brushless induction motor that runs quiet and lasts far longer than the universal motors in budget machines. For the most cleaning power, the Greenworks Pro GPW3000 at 3000 PSI genuinely handles concrete. For vehicles and delicate surfaces, the Karcher K5 Premium Smart Control is the safest and most refined.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are electric pressure washers powerful enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For siding, fences, decks, patio furniture, and vehicles, absolutely. A quality electric machine at 2000 PSI and above handles all of that comfortably and more safely than a high-output gas unit. Large flat concrete is where they fall short, because their 1.2 to 2.0 GPM water volume is well below a gas machine 2.4 to 3.5 GPM.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between induction and universal motors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Universal motors use carbon brushes, spin fast, and are light and cheap, but they run hot and loud and typically last a few seasons of regular use. Induction motors are brushless, heavier, quieter, run cool, and last for thousands of hours. Motor type is the strongest predictor of how long an electric pressure washer lasts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my pressure washer pulsing or losing pressure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always inadequate water supply causing cavitation. Check that the spigot is fully open, that the garden hose is at least 5/8-inch and not kinked or excessively long, and that the inlet screen is not clogged with grit. Purge air from the system by squeezing the trigger before starting the machine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to winterize an electric pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. There is no engine, but the pump still holds water, and water freezing inside cracks the housing, which is a terminal failure on most consumer machines. Draw pump saver antifreeze through the unit after the last use of the season and store it above freezing where possible.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What PSI is safe for washing a car?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Around 1,500 PSI or less with a 40-degree nozzle, held at least two feet from the surface, and never aimed directly at seals, badges, or trim. Machines with surface-specific pressure control, like the Karcher Smart Control system, set an appropriate level automatically and make accidental damage much less likely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does PSI Max mean on a pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                PSI Max and GPM Max are marketing figures some brands measure inside the machine rather than at the nozzle, so you never experience them while cleaning. Look instead for PWMA or CETA certification, which means an independent standard governs how the pressure and flow figures were measured and reported.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are cleaning units on a pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cleaning units are PSI multiplied by GPM, and they are the single best number for comparing two machines because they account for both pressure and water volume. Most electric pressure washers land between 2,800 and 6,000 cleaning units, while entry-level gas machines start around 6,000.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Electric Pressure Washer Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric machines fail in predictable ways, and none of the causes are the electrical side that people worry about.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Motor Type Entirely</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two machines with identical PSI figures can have completely different service lives depending on whether the motor is induction or universal. Listings often omit it, and a very light machine with high claimed pressure is almost certainly universal. Paying more for induction is the single best value decision in this category.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starving the Pump With a Long, Narrow Hose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pressure washer pump must receive its full rated volume, and a long, narrow, or kinked garden hose cannot supply it. The pump then draws air, cavitates, and erodes its own seals. Use a 5/8-inch or larger hose no longer than about 25 feet from a fully open spigot, and clean the inlet screen regularly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying to Apply Soap Through a High-Pressure Nozzle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The detergent siphon only engages at low pressure, which is why the black soap nozzle exists. Using any other tip means no detergent will be drawn through, and people routinely conclude the soap system is broken. Apply with the black tip, let it dwell without drying, then switch tips to rinse.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Winterization Because There Is No Engine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The absence of an engine leads people to store electric machines without any preparation, but the pump still holds water, and one freeze cracks the housing permanently. Run pump antifreeze through it at the end of the season and store it somewhere above freezing. It takes five minutes and saves the machine.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

